Savlovschi Andrei-BOGDAN, 311CB

The main function starts by allocating variables and then scanning
each command. Depending on the command, additional data is scanned
(file locations, positions, pixel colors, etc.).
At the end the resources are free'd. 

Since the undo and redo commands are relatively similar, and are found
in all the tasks, I will cover them at the end. This also includes the
"last_cmd" function and almost every one that has the word "stack"
in it.

The program makes use of three structs: LSYSTEM, FONT and TURTLE_STATE
which are used to save the data or the state of the program.


Task 1: "Grafeme fractale"


In task one, we are introduced with the first commands: LSYSTEM and DERIVE.

1.1: LSYSTEM
The lsystem function makes use of a struct with the same name, LSYSTEM (for 
reference, all my commands are in lowercase while structs are in uppercase).

The function starts by opening a file pointer to the file_location
provided by input, and checks if the file exists.
If it exists, it starts reading the data.

At the end if in 'n'- normal mode, it will display that the command was
successful.

1.2: DERIVE
The derive function starts by checking if the lsystem is loaded.
If it is loaded, it allocates space for two stings. One will serve as
the axiom and the other will be the derivation of the axiom.

Each character of the axiom will be checked if it represents a symbol,
in which case the successor of it will be added at the end of the derived
string. If a character is not a symbol, it will be added alone to the end
of the derived array.
While this processes is happening, the size of the derived array is stored.
Before it reaches the point where it is full, the array will be
reallocated with a larger size.

The last lines of code free's one of strings and returns the other.
The output will be printed and then the last string will be free'd as well.



Task 2: "Turtle graphics"


The second task introduces 3 commands: LOAD, TURTLE and SAVE.

2.1: LOAD
Similarly to LSYSTEM command, "load" checks if the file exists.

It will skip the first 3 characters ("P6\n") and start reading dimensions.
read_dimesions transforms each digit from a char format to an int format
and forms the width and height, until either a space or an endline is read.

After that , it will read the rgb values and transform them into an int format.
Each color represented by an int will have the value
color = red * 256^2 + green * 256 + blue, making the rgb values occupy 1 byte
each for a total of 3.
The colors of the pixels are each saved into an 2d int array.

The message for successful loading is displayed in the end.

2.2: TURTLE
In the beginning, the values for all the parameters of the command are scanned.
Both the lsystem and image (load function) are checked to be loaded.

From here, all the things happening are thoroughly explained in the task.

Because it was never showed (at least to me, beside generic descriptions) how
a proper readme should be made, and I didn't receive yet the manual grading
for the second homework to know what I could do better, I will explain again
what was already told by the task since that is what the turtle function does.

I will mark this part with * in case you want to skip it.

*********************************************

The axiom is derived to obtain the command for the turtle function
and TURTLE_STATE array is initialized, TURTLE_STATE being the 'S' array
in the task. This array is present in three functions, add_state, get_state
and remove_state. 

add_state adds the turtle position and orientation to the end of the
TURTLE_STATE array

get_state gets the last position and orientation of the turtle saved

remove_state removes the last position and orientation of the turtle

The 'F' character in the command calculates the ending position of the
turtle depending on the distance of each step and the angle. A line is
drawn between the starting end ending positions, using the algorithm
already provided, in which the values are rounded to the nearest int value.

The '+' character adds the angle to the current orientation of the turtle
and '-' subtracts it.

The '[' character calls add_state with the function expressed earlier.

The ']' character replaces the current position and orientation with
the last state saved and removes it.

*********************************************

At the end, "Drawing done" is printed.

2.3: SAVE
The name of the file which is supposed to be saved is read and the ppm
is created.

The signature is written into the document, followed by the dimensions
which are transformed back to ascii representations.
Following, the channel max value is also written (255).

The colors in the 2d int array are transformed back to rgb values and written
in order.

The message printed in the end signifies the success of the command.



TASK 3: "FONT"


The third task asks us to integrate two commands: FONT and TYPE.

3.1: FONT

Unlike the other functions, I also made font allocate all the arrays
since each font requires a different amount of memory.

It first verifies if the font file exits then it scans the font's name.
As the name isn't limited to a specific number of letters and symbols,
it is scanned character by character and the array is reallocated in
case it doesn't fit in the initial size.

The next process is to get all the other usefull data in the font,
done by using "scan_and_alloc_char_info". The function scans all the words
until it gets to "CHARS", where the number of characters in the font
is located.

"allocate_font" is dependend on the number of characters in the font
and that is why the allocation takes part inside the function as opposed
to in main.

The useless data (such as the the names for the type of data) is scanned
separately into the "useless_info" array.

The bitmap in the FONT struct is a character 2d array, as the task requires
me to read only values in the ascii table (it could be easily changed to an int
array for an extended number of symbols). While it may be disguised as a
2d array, it is actually a "compressed" 3d one. On every line sits the bitmap
for a specific character. The actual bitmap is stored like a computer would
do it in memory, by making every row sit next to the other.

It may seem like an unnecessary step which may as well be, but it is
easier to comprehend than allocating and free'ing a 3d array.
(I already done so in a laboratory, which is exactly why I don't want
to make it here).

"scan_hex_to_char" gets the hex values and transforms them into ints that
are in turn returned as character values.

After it reads all the useful data, the succes message is printed and
the font is marked as loaded.

3.2: TYPE

The "type" function is a bit special to me as I've managed to make every
letter be in almost all orientations and positions possible before
getting them right.

As usual, "type" scans all the command parameters and checks for
the font and image to be loaded.

It then searches for the location the letter is stored in the font,
writes it on the image, and increments the positions according to the
ones specified in the file.

The "write_char" calculates the row size of the bitmap, and then gets
each char stored in it. For each char, the value of the bits is extracted
and colored on the image, only if the value is 1 and the pixel is in bounds.

If the operation succeeds, the message is printed.



TASK 4: "GRAVURA"


Task 4 introduces us to a single command: BITCHECK

4.1: BITCHECK

Bitcheck operates in a quite simple matter: it gets each int representing
a color from the array pixels, then gets every group of 4 bits, a group
which I gave the name halfbyte, since it literally has half of the bits
from the byte.

Then the halfbyte is compared with the values 13 and 2 (represented in
binary as 1101 and 0010). If it is equal to one of them, the warning is
printed.

After the first pixel, the last 3 bits are stored as a remainder and get
placed right in front of the next color's bits. Since each color is
stored using only 3 bytes of the int, placing the bits in front does
not affect the color of the pixel.

There is a special case where the remainder and the next bit from
the colors forms one of the expected sequences, in which case the warning
is displayed for the last pixel since that is where the "special bit"
is located.



UNDO and REDO


In each undoable function there will be the "last_cmd" function.
"last_cmd" both stores characters representing letters for each
undoable command (such as 'F' for FONT and 'L' for LSYSTEM),
this also including both UNDO and REDO commands.

It has a static character pointer, for which memory is allocated
at the start of main using the action 'a'. The memory is reallocated
in case there are more commands than the size, and free'd at the end of
program using the action 'f'.

The '?' action makes the function return the last command.

The '-' action makes the function return the last undoable command.
This means that if a command is already undo-ed, it will skip it.

On the other hand, the '+' action makes the function return the last
redoable command.

Both '-' and '+' action make use of the fact that the UNDO and REDO
functions are part of the stack and, depending on them, the stack
is potentially searched further back.

Any other character that is passed in the function is saved on the stack.


Similar to "last_cmd" function, there are "lsys_file_location_stack",
"font_file_location_stack" and "ppm_file_location_stack".

All of them are allocated and free'd at the start and end of main
respectively, using 'a' and 'f' as parameters.

They are used to store the state of the program, marked by the action 's'.
While "ppm_file_location_stack" stores all the data of the image,
the other two just save the location of the file, which can be used
by the LSYSTEM and FONT functions to load the files again in the program,
thus saving some memory.

Depending on the "last_cmd" function, the "undo" and "redo" functions call
the stack functions with either 'u' for undo and 'r' for redo. That causes
the counter, a static int in the functions, to move upwards or downwards
in the stack.

In "lsys_file_location_stack" and "font_file_location_stack"
the file locations of the loaded structs are changed. The LSYSTEM and
FONT functions are called again, causing them to be loaded. For the undo
function, they are called with the mode 'u', which causes them to not
display messages.

"ppm_file_location_stack" is a bit more complicated. This function also
needs to keep track the range where the images are stored, so they can
all be free'd in the end. Depending on the action, the image is copied
into the stack or from the stack.

"copy_ppm", besides from just copying the data, can both free and
allocate a new pixel 2d array if needed, since the images can
have different dimensions or not be allocated in the first place,
such as in a failed loading.

"redo" checks the conditions for each of the functions
that modify the image using "ppm_file_location_stack", and displays
the failure or the success message of the commands.


